title ALES69 — Lotus's multi-tap delay in real time, taps named in milliseconds
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first, take the next agent-doable lap, send each finished increment, stop at custody gates
think ALES68 opened the multi-tap delay but named each tap's delay in sample indices; the family's established pattern gives each index-named rung a real-time twin (fade_ms, place_at, marker_time, and one rung ago echo_ms over the feedback echo). The multi-tap wants the same face.
think Crux-first, this is the small high-confidence twin rather than the larger fractional-delay primitive the modulated delay (chorus/flanger) will want; it mirrors echo_ms exactly, so it is the tractable next lap that keeps the family symmetric.
think Built it as its own module multitap_time.rye (ALES69) mirroring echo_time.rye being a separate module — additive, touches neither ALES5 nor ALES68. TapMs{delay_ms,num,den} converts each delay through clock.samples_for into an index-named Tap in a bounded stack buffer, then delegates to multitap unchanged.
think The count is checked against max_taps BEFORE any conversion so the stack buffer always holds them and the clip is untouched on a bad count; the grounding law is the clock adds only the units, so the ms-named multi-tap equals the index-named multi-tap byte-for-byte.
obs GREEN on the first build across six clauses — no RED this round (the max_clip sizing lesson from ALES67 was already in hand, so case 2 used a 240-sample / 5 ms clip from the start). At 48 kHz 1 ms = 48 samples, the copy sitting exactly at index 48 at half the impulse.
obs Independence carried into real time: the 4 ms copy is 1/4 of the ORIGINAL 8000 (= 2000), not a copy-of-a-copy — the ALES68 property proven through the clock.
loom witness=ales_multitap_time_witness cases=6 green=1 reruns=ales68_taps max_clip=4096 max_taps=16
file lotus/multitap_time.rye ALES69 — multitap_ms names each tap's delay in milliseconds, a thin twin over ALES68 through the ALES5 clock
